# Icons and images

Do not use images to decorate a dashboard. Add an image only when the household has a real need for it, such as finding the reset button on the boiler.

Make sure someone who cannot see an image still gets all of its information. Think about people with low vision too, and the tools they use, such as a screen reader, a screen magnifier or a high contrast setting.

This guidance is for people who build Home Assistant dashboards and Civica pages. To word the alt text itself, see [Images](../content/formatting-content/images.html) in the writing guide.

## Using images to give information

Dashboards usually work best without relying on images. Write clear names and states first, such as “Front door” and “Locked”.

An image can sometimes help people follow an instruction, such as where the pairing button is on a new sensor. But an image is only a visual aid. The text on the dashboard must hold everything people need to check or control their home.

If you add an image, decide whether it needs alt text. [Alt text](images-alt-text.html) explains how to decide.

## Choose the right kind of image

| Kind | Use it to | Guidance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Photograph | Show what a real thing looks like, such as the stopcock under the sink | [Photography](images-photography.html) |
| Camera image | Show what is happening at a place now, such as the front door | [Cameras, streams and snapshots](../home-assistant/cameras.html) |
| Illustration | Explain something complex in a simple way, such as how to pair a sensor | [Illustrations](images-illustrations.html) |
| Icon | Help people recognise a device, room or action they already know by name | [Icons](images-icons.html) |
| Floor plan or diagram | Show where rooms and devices are in the home | [Avoid images that contain text](images-text.html#floor-plans-and-diagrams) |
| Chart | Show how a reading changes over time | [Data visualisation](brand-data.html) |

